WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.449 --> 00:00:09.490
HEATHER CROSS: Hi, there.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:10.421
Heather, again.

00:00:10.421 --> 00:00:11.920
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Hi, I'm Priyanka.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.980
You've probably seen me on
GCP Live YouTube series.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.350
I'm also a customer
engineer here at Google.

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.740
As a CE, I help companies
understand their big data.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.440
Having access to big
data adds no value

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.884
unless there's a way to use it.

00:00:24.884 --> 00:00:26.050
HEATHER CROSS: That's right.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.810
Big data requires
a way to perform

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.150
big queries, which brings us to
the topic of today's episode,

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:31.990
Google BigQuery.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.190
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Today's data
is complex, and handling it

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:43.310
requires a massive investment
in system architecture

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:44.390
and hardware.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.040
It doesn't end there, though.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.770
You need strategies
for scalability.

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:51.770
Then, it needs to be
managed and maintained.

00:00:51.770 --> 00:00:54.560
The result, a system
where queries can still

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.452
take minutes to hours to run.

00:00:56.452 --> 00:00:58.160
HEATHER CROSS: But
what's more important,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.530
developing the infrastructure
or finding insights

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:01.704
from your data?

00:01:01.704 --> 00:01:04.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well, this is
where Google BigQuery comes in.

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:07.850
It's a fully-managed,
massive-scale, low-cost

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.010
enterprise data
warehouse, running

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.010
on top of Google's
proven compute, storage,

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:14.785
and networking infrastructure.

00:01:14.785 --> 00:01:16.910
HEATHER CROSS: So by
replacing the typical hardware

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.280
setup for a traditional
data warehouse,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.780
with the BigQuery
service, there's

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.380
no infrastructure to manage,
no database administrator,

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.120
and you can focus
on analyzing data

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.505
to find meaningful insights
using familiar SQL.

00:01:33.889 --> 00:01:35.430
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
BigQuery can serve

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.310
as a collective whole
for all analytical data

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:39.930
in your organization.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.170
BigQuery is fantastic for
running ad hoc queries

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.590
and aggregating queries across
extremely large data sets.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.090
HEATHER CROSS: And
it's really fast.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.930
It can scan terabytes in seconds
and petabytes in minutes.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.540
This makes interactive
self-service exploration

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.940
of massive data
sets viable, which

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.580
means better analysis,
more creativity,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.410
and you can drive more
interesting insights

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:02.630
from your data.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:05.370
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: BigQuery does
not replace every enterprise

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:06.510
data stored, though.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.470
For instance, it's not an online
transaction processing system.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.990
And it's not geared
towards applying changes

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.560
as they happen.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.310
Since BigQuery is a
self-contained, cloud-based

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.590
solution, it's also not
an on-premise solution.

00:02:24.961 --> 00:02:26.710
HEATHER CROSS: Query
and storage resources

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.010
are allocated dynamically
based on your usage patterns.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:31.320
Got a really big query?

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.060
BigQuery scales for you,
using the processing power

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.250
of Google's infrastructure.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.740
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Sharing
and collaboration are easy.

00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:42.310
You can control access to
both the project and your data

00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:45.460
based on your business
needs, such as giving others

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.430
the ability to view
or query your data.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:51.220
And because you can use
standard SQL queries,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.170
anyone can get involved.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:55.720
HEATHER CROSS: Replicating data
across multiple geographies

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.810
ensures a 99.9% SLA.

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.980
You're always going to be
able to access your data.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.650
And you won't lose your data.

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:06.310
BigQuery also encrypts all
data at rest and in-transit

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:07.235
by default.

00:03:07.235 --> 00:03:08.860
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
In terms of pricing,

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:12.580
BigQuery separates the concepts
of storage and compute.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.510
This allows you to scale and
pay for each independently.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.170
You can either choose a
pay-as-you-go model or a flat

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:21.010
rate monthly price.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.870
HEATHER CROSS: Now, the
fun part, Qwik Labs.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:30.786
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: You
can check out the links

00:03:30.786 --> 00:03:32.440
to start the Qwik
Labs yourself here.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:34.770
These labs provide an
introductory walkthrough

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:38.370
of how to load and query
data using both BigQuery web

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.810
UI and the command line tool.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.320
Keep in mind that these labs
will take about 30 minutes each

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:45.264
to complete.

00:03:45.264 --> 00:03:46.930
HEATHER CROSS: At
this point in the lab,

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.840
we've loaded a custom data
set into the new table.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:53.710
We're going to preview the table
and query the custom data set.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:56.140
Locate the table in BigQuery.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:59.800
Open the table and click
Preview to view the data.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.810
Click Compose Query.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.510
Query the babynames data set.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.310
In this demo, the query
displays the top five boy

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.180
names for 2014.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.410
Well, that's all the
time we have for today.

00:04:12.410 --> 00:04:14.740
We hope you enjoyed this
episode on BigQuery.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:16.073
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: That's right.

